                <pb facs="0245" n="214" sameAs="frus:frus1981-88v44p1/pg214"/>

                <head>64. Memorandum From William Wright and Sven Kraemer of the National Security
                    Council Staff to the President’s Assistant for National Security Affairs
                        (McFarlane)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d64-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1985-10-03">October 3, 1985</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>SACG, Friday, October 4, 4:30–5:30 P.M.</item>
                </list>
                <p><hi rendition="#italic">Purpose</hi>. This package provides the agenda and some
                    supporting material for the SACG meeting scheduled for Friday, October 4.
                    Because each of the agenda items is currently in a fluid state an update
                    including your talking points will be provided tomorrow morning.</p>
                <p><hi rendition="#italic">Objectives</hi>. The objectives of this SACG meeting are
                    to: <list>
                        <label>1.</label>
                        <item>(<hi rendition="#italic">Agenda Item 2—Paris Developments</hi>) The
                            Gorbachev announcements today from Paris,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d64-f2"/>
                            revealed new elements at the Soviet <pb facs="0246" n="215"
                                xml:id="pg215"/> propaganda campaign in Europe. A short five-minute
                            assessment of the new elements and our response will be useful to keep
                            everyone up to date and solicit comments. A preliminary set of press Qs
                            and As is at <hi rendition="#italic">Tab C</hi>.<anchor n="3"
                                xml:id="d64-f3"/></item>
                        <label>2.</label>
                        <item>(<hi rendition="#italic">Agenda Item 3—Special Paper on Options</hi>)
                            As directed, the Support Group has produced a rough draft based on the
                            outline at <hi rendition="#italic">Tab D</hi>.<anchor n="4"
                                xml:id="d64-f4"/> Your talking points will support a discussion
                            focused on the outline only—we expect to have a more mature paper
                            completed this weekend.</item>
                        <label>3.</label>
                        <item>(<hi rendition="#italic">Agenda Item 4—ABM Treaty Terminology</hi>)
                            The Support Group has produced a draft decision document (<hi
                                rendition="#italic">Tab E</hi>) but OSD participation has been
                            limited by a fundamental belief, as expressed by Fred Ikle in today’s
                            memorandum to you (<hi rendition="#italic">Tab F</hi>) that no decision
                            be taken pending a complete legal and technical analysis. The issue
                            needs a decision because failure to agree to a standard set of
                            terminology gives the impression that we are hedging; spokesmen and
                            negotiators are hesitant to reaffirm existing interpretations for fear
                            that it will prejudice the outcome.</item>
                        <label>4.</label>
                        <item>(<hi rendition="#italic">Agenda Item 5—Pending Actions</hi>) You asked
                            Ken Adelman to be prepared to lead a discussion on the intended use of
                            the “transition” paper and Mike Guhin is prepared to do so in his stead.
                            Also, Fred Ikle, who objected strongly to the ABM Treaty “erosion”
                            paper, at <hi rendition="#italic">Tab G</hi>, is prepared to offer views
                            on why that paper should not be approved for use.</item>
                    </list></p>
                <p><hi rendition="#italic">Agenda and Talking Points</hi>, The agenda (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Tab A</hi>)<anchor n="5" xml:id="d64-f5"/> and proposed
                    talking points (to be provided) (<hi rendition="#italic">Tab B</hi>)<anchor
                        n="6" xml:id="d64-f6"/> are to support your chairmanship of the meeting.</p>
                <p rendition="#section-heading #italic">Recommendation</p>
                <p>That you use the approach outlined above, and the supporting agenda (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Tab A</hi>) and talking points (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Tab B</hi>), in conducting the SACG on Friday, October 4.<anchor n="7"
                        xml:id="d64-f7"/></p>
                <p>Concurrence: Ron Lehman, Bob Linhard, Steve Steiner and Jack Matlock<anchor n="8"
                        xml:id="d64-f8"/></p>









